description:
Minimal repro (works in txt/dot parse, panics in every renderer):

```
digraph g {
"a" -> "t1" [label="x"];
"a" -> "t2" [label="x"];
"a" -> "t3" [label="x"];
"a" -> "t4" [label="x"];
}
```

`gently min.dot --as ascii` panics: `layout.rs:468` `attempt to subtract with overflow` in `label_cells` — `path[(path.len() - 1) / 2]` underflows because the labelled edge has an EMPTY routed path in `edge_paths`. Unlabeled fan-out 4 does NOT panic (label_cells only indexes labelled edges); fan-out 3 with labels is fine. So: labelled edge + >=4 out of one node → some slot-routed edge gets a zero-length path.

This blocks every downstream consumer that always labels edges — e.g. spk (specodelic) emits `label=` on every DOT edge, so its full corpus projection (337 edges) and even a single spec file (44 edges) panic in both `--as ascii` and `--as html`. Discovered while evaluating gently as the external renderer for specodelic graph views (their D8 explicitly pipes into graph-easy/gently).

Suggested fix: guard `path.is_empty()` in label_cells (and find why the route is empty for fan-out >= 4 — the real bug is upstream of the indexing).

Rendering a DOT projection that contains a directed cycle errors with:

    gently: render error: edge 1 enters its box through an unsupported side
    -> Run: gently doctor

Repro: specodelic wiring-view DOT (4 nodes, includes a 2-cycle between files):

```
digraph wiring {
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, style=rounded, fontsize=11];
  edge [color=gray30, arrowsize=0.7];
  "ge.ascii_render" -> "ge.layout" [label="1 satisfies"];
  "ge.boxart_render" -> "ge.layout" [label="1 satisfies"];
  "ge.html_render" -> "ge.layout" [label="1 satisfies"];
  "ge.layout" -> "ge.graph_model" [label="1 satisfies"];
}
```

Note: that file renders FINE — the panicking variant is specodelic corpus wiring which has an actual back-edge (cycle) between two files (their satisfies graph is mutual: consumer satisfies producer and vice versa). Re-export the failing DOT to attach a cleaner repro: `spk graph openspec/specs --view wiring --format dot` run inside the specodelic repo, saved at the time of filing as /tmp/w2.dot (regenerate there).

Real-world impact: dependency graphs (which is what corpus graphs ARE) routinely contain cycles; Graph::Easy upstream handles them. Until supported, gently cannot render any cyclic dependency graph.

Found while evaluating gently as the external renderer for specodelic graph views.
